.TH CLEANER 1 "2026-10-19" "linux-cleaner 1.7.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
cleaner \- safely clean cache and temporary files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cleaner
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B cleaner
is a configuration-driven Bash utility that estimates and reclaims disk space by
cleaning caches, temporary files, and logs. It runs in dry-run mode by default,
routes every deletion through a path-safety check, blocks system directories,
allows paths outside the user's
.BR $HOME ,
refuses to follow symlinks, and can move files to a restorable quarantine instead
of deleting them permanently.
After the savings total,
.B cleaner
prints a plan report: run mode, risk ceiling, config path, counts per status,
reclaimable bytes by risk, the largest targets, and skipped targets.
When selected targets exceed the risk ceiling, the risk totals are followed by
\(lqAbove \-\-risk LEVEL (not analysed): medium=N danger=M; preview with \-\-risk LEVEL\(rq
and one \(lqHidden by risk: name, ...\(rq line.
These targets are counted and named after name/path filters, without size analysis;
they do not contribute to reclaimable bytes. Both lines are omitted when none are hidden.
JSON and metrics are unchanged.
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses the plan report. After cleanup, a result report lists cleaned,
skipped, and failed counts, bytes quarantined versus permanently reclaimed,
the quarantine session path when one was created, and elapsed time.
A trash cleanup that quarantined something then prints
\(lqUndo: cleaner \-\-restore SESSION\(rq (not under
.BR \-\-quiet ,
and not in the saved report).
.B \-\-json
includes neither report.
A non-dry-run cleanup also saves both reports, with a timestamp and
without color codes, under the state directory (see
.BR FILES ).
.B \-\-quiet
hides the plan on the screen; the saved copy still includes it.
.B \-\-report
prints that file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Use a custom configuration file instead of the resolved default.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-dry\-run
Calculate and display potential space savings without deleting anything (default).
With
.BR \-\-restore
or
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
preview recovery or session deletion instead (see below).
A text preview that found something to clean ends with a
.B Next:
line. A bare preview names
.BR "cleaner \-\-clean" .
When the preview used an explicit config, a risk above safe,
.BR \-\-trash ,
.BR \-\-exclude ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
or
.B \-\-trash\-keep\-days
(from the command line or their environment variables), the next line
prints the full apply command with those options, each value quoted with
.BR "printf %q" ,
so it applies the same plan.
.B \-\-quiet
and
.B \-\-json
print no
.B Next:
line.
.TP
.B \-\-clean
Perform cleanup with a summary confirmation prompt. When
.BR flock (1)
is available, mutating runs
.RB ( \-\-clean / \-\-force / \-\-interactive ,
.BR \-\-restore ,
and
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions )
take an exclusive lock on
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.lock
and fail immediately on contention by default.
.B \-\-lock\-wait
opts in to bounded waiting; dry-run and read-only modes never take the lock.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-interactive
Enable cleanup and prompt for confirmation before cleaning each target.
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-force
Clean immediately without confirmation prompts (except danger level).
.TP
.B \-\-yes\-danger
Bypass the manual confirmation prompt for danger-risk targets.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trash
Select quarantine instead of permanent deletion. This flag alone keeps the
default preview mode. When a target sits on a different filesystem from the
quarantine directory,
.B cleaner
checks free space on the quarantine filesystem first and refuses the target
when its planned size exceeds available space, leaving it in place;
same-filesystem moves are renames and need no check.
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-risk " " \fILEVEL\fR
Maximum risk level to clean: safe, medium, or danger (default: safe).
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-exclude " " \fIPATTERNS\fR
Comma-separated glob patterns to exclude from cleanup.
.TP
.B \-\-allow\-outside\-home
Deprecated no-op. Targets outside
.BR $HOME
are always allowed. Prints one warning on stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-log \fR[\fIFILE\fR]
Append an operation log (opt-in, off by default; kept under
.BR $HOME ).
Records cleanup outcomes and, for
.B \-\-restore
and
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
one line per restored target or purged session.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress per-target status lines and the decorative header; errors, total
savings, and the final summary still print.
.TP
.B \-\-debug
Show per-target FOUND, CLEAN and PROTECTED lines (suppressed by default; overridden by
.BR \-\-quiet ).
.TP
.B \-\-show\-matches
In a text dry run, show per-target status lines and indent eligible paths below
each found ordinary target. Directory listings use the same age and exclusion
predicates as cleanup; single files apply their age limit. Without age or
exclusions, print only the target's own path. Skips action:* targets;
.B \-\-quiet
suppresses the listing. CLI-only (no environment mirror). Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-clean ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-interactive
(even with a later
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
.BR \-\-json ,
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-exit\-code
In a text or JSON cleanup dry run, exit 2 when cleanup would run (JSON
.B would_skip
is null), or 0 when below
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
at or above
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
or there is nothing to clean. Errors still exit 1. CLI-only (no environment
mirror). The intentional status 2 emits no journal error priority line and is
not carried into the
.B Next:
command. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-clean ,
.BR \-\-force ,
.BR \-\-interactive
(even with a later
.BR \-\-dry\-run ),
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-fail\-fast
With cleanup: after the first failed target's accounting and log entry, stop
before the next target and skip inline retention, even if the last target failed.
The run exits 1 and the printed and saved result report includes
.BR "Stopped early: \-\-fail-fast after <name>; <N> targets not attempted" .
Safety skips and blocked targets do not trigger a stop. Without this option,
cleanup and retention continue after target failures as before. Dry-run performs
no cleanup and carries this option in its
.B Next:
command. Notifications and metrics are unchanged. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-notify
With cleanup: send one desktop notification via
.BR notify\-send (1)
after inline retention summarizing the cleanup result (first line of the result report plus its
.B Reclaimed:
line, and any
.B Interrupted:
or
.B "Retention failed: true"
line;
.B \-\-urgency=critical
when any target or inline retention failed or the run was interrupted). If an opted-in cleanup exits non-zero before reaching its result notification, sends one critical notification with
.B "Cleanup stopped with exit status \fIN\fB before finishing; see journalctl \-\-user \-t linux\-cleaner."
No-op on dry-run. Prints one warning on stderr when
.B \-\-notify
is active and
.B notify\-send
is not installed; a missing binary, missing session bus, or failing or hanging
notifier never changes the exit status. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-json
With
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
emit a machine-readable JSON summary instead of human-formatted output
.RB ( total_reclaimable_bytes ,
.BR total_reclaimable_human ,
.BR would_skip ,
per-target
.BR bytes / size_human / description / min_age_days / size_at_run_time / reason ).
The top-level
.B would_skip
names the gate a cleanup run would stop at
.RB ( skipped_below_min_reclaim ,
.BR skipped_at_or_above_min_free ,
or
.BR nothing_to_clean ,
checked in that order), or is
.B null
when cleanup would proceed; runtime-sized prune actions count as cleanable.
With
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
emit a JSON inventory of configured targets
.RB ( config ,
per-target
.BR name / risk / path / description ,
.BR min_age_days / check_processes / custom_action ).
With
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
emit a JSON inventory of quarantine sessions
.RB ( sessions
array of
.BR timestamp / size_bytes / size_human / date / unrecorded_entry_count / held / targets ).
With
.BR \-\-report ,
emit a JSON summary of the saved last cleanup report
.RB ( saved ,
.BR result
as
.BR completed ", " nothing_to_clean ", " skipped_below_min_reclaim ", or " skipped_at_or_above_min_free ,
.BR cleaned / skipped / failed / elapsed_seconds ,
.BR failed_targets ,
.BR quarantine_session ,
.BR interrupted ,
.BR retention_failed ,
.BR quarantined_bytes / permanent_bytes ).
The additive
.B retention_failed
field is true when inline retention failed, false when it did not fail
(including cancellation), and null when no retention outcome was saved.
Cleanup-target counts are unchanged.
The byte fields are exact accounted integers, or null for historical or malformed
byte records. New nothing-to-clean, minimum-reclaim and minimum-free skips
record zero.
Ordinary targets and system actions credit planned sizes; user prune actions
credit free-space deltas clamped at zero. These estimates do not guarantee
physically freed blocks. Interrupted runs include only already-credited work;
retention purge volume is excluded. Cancelling cleanup before work preserves
the previous report.
Implies
.BR \-\-quiet .
.TP
.B \-\-report
Print the saved last cleanup report and exit. Read-only; requires no
configuration file. Prints
.B "No cleanup report yet."
on stderr and exits 1 when the file is absent. With
.BR \-\-json ,
emit a machine-readable JSON summary instead of plain text. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-check\-health \fISECONDS\fR
Requires
.BR \-\-report ;
keep the normal text or JSON output, then exit 0 only for a saved successful result
whose file modification time is at most SECONDS old. Skipped targets,
nothing-to-clean, minimum-reclaim and minimum-free skips count as success.
Missing, unreadable, symlinked, stale, future-dated, failed (failed>0), interrupted,
retention-failed (Retention failed: true), or unrecognized reports exit 1 with
one reason line on stderr. Unavailable age information also fails. Symlinked
state directories are refused. Decimal integer 0..9223372036854775807 seconds;
leading zeros are normalized and the last occurrence wins. CLI-only, without
an environment mirror, config requirement, timer dependency, or JSON schema change.
Without this flag, readable saved outcomes still return 0 regardless of success.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
.BR \-\-doctor ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
.BR \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-fail\-fast ,
.BR \-\-show\-matches ,
or
.BR \-\-exit\-code .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-sessions
List quarantine sessions (read-only) and exit. Each session is shown with its
timestamp, size, and date, followed by the targets it holds: the type
.RB ( full
or
.BR aged )
and original path of every quarantined item, so a session's contents can be
inspected before it is restored or permanently purged.
With
.BR \-\-json ,
emit a machine-readable inventory instead of human-formatted output.
Every session includes integer
.B unrecorded_entry_count
in JSON; human output warns when it is positive. It counts immediate entries
(excluding quarantine.meta and the .hold marker) not equal to a trimmed recorded relative path or its
literal first component, including hidden entries and symlinks without following
them. Missing metadata accounts for no entries. Referenced-root descendants and
aliases are not inspected. Zero does not certify metadata integrity, recovery or
safe purge. Positive counts do not change confirmation or force policy. Failed
metadata reads or entry enumeration stop inventory and purge before confirmation
or deletion, including forced purge and inline retention.
A session protected by
.B \-\-hold\-session
ends its human line with
.B held
and has
.B held
set to true in JSON (false otherwise). Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-list\-targets
List targets defined in the configuration file
.RB ( NAME ", " risk ", " path ", " description )
without analyzing disk usage or modifying anything. NAME selection with
.BR \-\-only\-target
and
.BR \-\-exclude\-target
uses the same matching and empty-selection errors as cleanup.
.B \-\-only\-path
and
.B \-\-exclude\-path
select or skip physical paths with the same rules as cleanup. Inventory includes
all risk levels. Read-only; requires a configuration file. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-check\-config
Validate the configuration file with the same structural checks as a cleanup
run (field count, required fields, CHECK_PROCESSES shape, MIN_AGE_DAYS,
known actions, known risks) and exit without disk analysis. Prints
.B OK: N targets in <path>
on success. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-init\-config
Copy the active configuration file to
.I ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:\-$HOME/.config}/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf
(mode
.BR 0644 ,
never overwriting an existing file or following symlinks) and exit. Cannot be
combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-doctor ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
.BR \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-fail\-fast ,
or
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-doctor
Print a read-only checklist of the runtime environment a cleanup depends on
.RB ( OK / WARN / INFO
lines for the resolved configuration path and whether it came from XDG, /etc,
checkout, or explicit;
.BR check_gnu_userland ;
.BR pgrep ;
.BR flock ;
.BR git ;
non-interactive
.BR "sudo \-n" ;
.BR notify\-send ;
.B "systemctl \-\-user is\-enabled"
for the clean, retention, and monitor timers; the last cleanup outcome; a WARN when the enabled clean
timer's env file sets LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH without LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS or
an enabled retention timer; a WARN when
.I ~/.config/linux\-cleaner/monitor.env
is unreadable or sets both LINUX_CLEANER_ONLY_PATH and LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS;
and the quarantine session count).
After the timer states, prints
.B "INFO: last cleanup: <Saved> <Result>"
using the Saved and Result header values from
.IR ${XDG_STATE_HOME:-$HOME/.local/state}/linux\-cleaner/last\-report.txt ,
or
.B "INFO: last cleanup: none recorded"
when absent. Symlinked reports or linux-cleaner state directories are not followed.
Uses WARN instead of INFO when the result has failed>0, an Interrupted line is
present, a Retention failed: true line is present (appending
.BR "; retention failed" ),
or the clean timer is enabled and the report's modification time is
more than two days old. An unreadable report prints
.BR "WARN: last cleanup: report not readable" .
These warnings do not change the exit status.
When the clean timer is enabled, also prints
.B "INFO: daily timer plan: risk=<value|safe> deletion=<quarantine|permanent> keep-days=<N|none> [config=<path>] (<env path|no env file>)"
from
.IR ~/.config/linux\-cleaner/env .
Without an env file, the plan is risk=safe, deletion=permanent, keep-days=none.
config= appears when the env file sets LINUX_CLEANER_CONFIG.
The line uses WARN and appends
.B "; invalid risk"
or
.B "; config not readable"
when the env risk is not safe, medium, danger or dangerous (in any case), or that config is not a readable file;
.B "; invalid keep-days"
when LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS is not a non-negative integer; and
.B "; config invalid"
when that config fails the schema check; and
.B "; only-path with keep-days"
when the file sets both LINUX_CLEANER_ONLY_PATH and LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS, which the clean run refuses.
A relative LINUX_CLEANER_CONFIG is checked under HOME, where the user service starts.
Values are trimmed of surrounding whitespace, as systemd does.
An env file that exists but cannot be read prints
.B "WARN: daily timer plan: env file not readable (path)"
because systemd then fails every timer run.
No plan line is printed when the clean timer is disabled.
The retention warning and exit status are unchanged.
A readable file is schema-checked; an invalid file prints
.B "WARN: config: path (source, error)"
and the rest of the checklist still prints.
Exit 0 when cleanup can run or 1 when the configuration file is missing,
unreadable, or invalid, or GNU userland is missing. Requires no configuration file to print
the checklist. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
.BR \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-fail\-fast ,
or
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-only \fIPATTERN\fR
Used with
.BR \-\-restore :
restore only the quarantined targets whose original path contains
.IR PATTERN ;
every other target stays quarantined and the session is kept, with its metadata
rewritten so a later full
.B \-\-restore
of that session still succeeds. Exits 1 if no target matches, or if
.B \-\-only
is given without
.BR \-\-restore .
Cannot be mixed with
.BR \-\-only\-exact ;
repeating the same selector uses its last value.
.TP
.B \-\-only\-exact \fIPATH\fR
Used with
.BR \-\-restore :
select records whose original path equals the nonempty literal
.IR PATH .
Copy the path shown by session inventory (or its JSON targets[].path).
The argument is not trimmed, expanded or normalized and need not be absolute
or exist. Wildcards, descendants and comma lists have no special meaning.
Unselected targets stay quarantined for a later restore. Exits 1 on no match
or without
.BR \-\-restore .
Cannot be mixed with
.B \-\-only
in either order; repeating the same selector uses its last value.
This option has no environment mirror.
.TP
.B \-\-only\-target \fIPATTERN\fR
Used with cleanup or configured-target inventory: process only config targets whose
.B NAME
contains
.IR PATTERN
(case-sensitive literal substring match;
.I PATTERN
may be a comma-separated list: a target matches when its
.B NAME
contains any listed substring, and empty entries are rejected). Other targets are skipped. Exits 1 if no target name matches.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-target \fIPATTERN\fR
Used with cleanup or configured-target inventory: skip config targets whose
.B NAME
contains
.IR PATTERN
(case-sensitive literal substring match;
.I PATTERN
may be a comma-separated list: a target matches when its
.B NAME
contains any listed substring, and empty entries are rejected). Combined with
.B \-\-only\-target ,
both filters apply. Exits 1 if the filters leave no targets to process.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-only\-path \fIPATH\fR
With cleanup or configured-target inventory, select ordinary targets whose physical
path equals or lies below one existing absolute literal
.IR PATH .
Matching uses whole path components; / selects every eligible resolved target.
The last occurrence wins and overrides
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_ONLY_PATH .
Unset or empty environment input means no scope. Paths are literal, without
placeholder expansion. NAME filters apply
first, then PATH selection, then cleanup risk and safety guards. All action:*
rows are excluded because their effects may extend beyond the scope. Missing or
unresolvable candidates and expanded or physical paths containing line feeds are
ineligible.
An invalid scope exits 1. Existing NAME-selection errors take precedence over
no eligible PATH matches (also exit 1). Inventory preserves configured paths and includes all risks
without analysis. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
.BR \-\-doctor ,
or
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days
(including its environment mirror).
.TP
.B \-\-exclude\-path \fIPATH\fR
With cleanup or configured-target inventory, skip targets whose resolved physical
or expanded literal path equals, lies below, or contains one existing absolute literal
.IR PATH
(whole path components; action:* rows are excluded when their path matches;
missing targets that do not match literally are never excluded). The last occurrence wins and overrides
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_EXCLUDE_PATH ;
unset or empty environment input means no exclusion. Exits 1 if
.I PATH
is not a literal absolute path without line feeds, does not resolve to an
existing path, or removes every remaining target. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-lock\-wait \fISECONDS\fR
Wait up to a literal decimal 0..2147483647 seconds for the existing mutation lock
(default 0; leading zeros normalized). Zero retains immediate contention failure
and allows missing flock. Positive waits require flock and exit 1 on acquisition
failure before target, session, report or log effects; lock-file opening may occur.
Valid values are ignored by dry-run, inventories, report, check-config, init-config and doctor.
Last CLI value overrides inherited input. Missing/empty CLI and invalid effective
values fail before initialization or state creation. No retry loop is used.
.TP
.B \-\-min\-reclaim \fISIZE\fR
Used with cleanup: skip the run when the planned reclaimable total is below
.IR SIZE
(an integer with an optional
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", or " T
1024-based unit, such as
.B 500M
or
.BR 2G ).
User-level prune actions
.RB ( uv / pnpm / hf / nix\-store )
are sized at run time and do not count toward the threshold. In dry-run mode,
prints a note when a cleanup run would skip; in a cleanup run, prints why
nothing was cleaned, saves
.BI "Result: skipped below \-\-min\-reclaim " SIZE
in the last cleanup report, and exits 0. Exits 1 on a malformed
.IR SIZE .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-min\-free \fISIZE\fR
Used with cleanup: skip the run when the selected filesystem (default:
.BR HOME )
already has at least
.I SIZE
free (an integer with an optional
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", or " T
1024-based unit, such as
.BR 10G ;
.B 0
disables the check). Select another filesystem with
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path .
An unreadable free-space probe prints a warning and cleanup proceeds.
In dry-run mode, prints a note when a cleanup run would skip; in a cleanup
run, prints why nothing was cleaned, saves
.BI "Result: skipped at or above \-\-min\-free " SIZE
in the last cleanup report, and exits 0. Exits 1 on a malformed
.IR SIZE .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-min\-free\-path \fIPATH\fR
Select the filesystem measured by
.BR \-\-min\-free .
PATH must be an absolute existing file or directory. Requires
.B \-\-min\-free
or
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_FREE ;
a missing threshold, relative path, or nonexistent path exits 1.
Defaults to HOME when unset; CLI overrides
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_FREE_PATH .
A failed probe warns and cleanup proceeds. This selects the pressure probe,
not cleanup targets. Dry-run Next: commands carry both options; JSON
.B would_skip
and
.B \-\-exit\-code
use the same gate.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-min\-age \fIDAYS\fR
Used with cleanup: raise every ordinary target's
.B MIN_AGE_DAYS
threshold to at least
.I DAYS
days (a non-negative decimal integer) for that run. Targets configured with a
larger
.B MIN_AGE_DAYS
keep their configured threshold,
.B filter:cxx_build
rows use the raised value as their idle threshold, and
.B action:*
rows are skipped because external commands cannot apply a file-age filter. Exits
1 on a malformed
.I DAYS
or when no eligible config target remains after skipping
.B action:*
rows. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-action\-timeout \fISECONDS\fR
Used with cleanup: stop each
.B action:*
command that is still running after
.I SECONDS
seconds (a non-negative decimal integer) with
.BR "timeout \-\-kill\-after=10" ,
print that the action timed out, count its target as failed, and continue with
the remaining targets and retention, so the run exits 1.
.B 0
or unset keeps actions unbounded. A positive limit in a cleanup run requires
.BR timeout (1).
On a terminal the action runs in the foreground so Ctrl-C still reaches it; a
timeout then signals only the action's own process (sudo passes it on), so a
helper process the action started itself may outlive the limit.
Exits 1 on a malformed
.IR SECONDS .
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-trash\-keep\-days \fIDAYS\fR
Used with cleanup: purge quarantine sessions older than
.IR DAYS
days (a non-negative integer) at the end of a non-dry-run cleanup via
.BI older\-than: DAYS
retention (confirming unless
.B \-\-force
is set, and exiting 0 when no sessions match). A dry-run purges nothing. Exits 1
on a malformed
.IR DAYS
or if any session purge fails. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-restore \fISESSION\fR
Restore files from a quarantine session (latest or a timestamp).
Add an explicit
.B \-\-dry\-run
to print one outcome per selected original path:
\(lqwould restore\(rq, \(lqconflict\(rq, \(lqalready restored\(rq, \(lqmissing\(rq,
or \(lqunknown type\(rq (a record the real restore skips as failed),
followed by two spaces and the path.
The preview honours
.B \-\-only
and
.BR \-\-only\-exact ,
ends with \(lqDry-run: nothing restored.\(rq, and exits 0 even for conflicts or missing payload.
An unmatched selector still exits 1.
It takes no lock, invokes no sudo, and leaves payload, logs, metadata and session mtime unchanged.
Probes use the current user's access; a preview does not guarantee permissions or free space for a later restore.
Each target records one UID/GID pair, applied on a best-effort basis during
restore. Distinct ownership within a mixed-owner tree is not preserved.
A named session must be an immediate child of the quarantine directory;
path separators and
.BR . / ..
are rejected. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-purge\-sessions \fISESSION\fR
Permanently delete quarantine session(s) to reclaim space:
.IR latest ", " all ", " older\-than:DAYS ", " max\-size:SIZE ", " keep\-latest:N ", or a specific timestamp."
A named timestamp must likewise be an immediate child name (no path separators or
.BR . / .. ).
The
.I older\-than:DAYS
selector purges every session last modified more than
.I DAYS
days ago, and exits 0 when none match, so it is safe to run from cron as a
retention policy that keeps quarantine bounded. A malformed day count is an error.
The
.I max\-size:SIZE
selector walks sessions newest-first summing apparent byte sizes
.RB ( "du \-sb" )
and purges every session once the running total exceeds
.I SIZE
(an integer with an optional
.BR K ", " M ", " G ", or " T
1024-based unit, as for
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ),
keeping the newest sessions within the budget; it exits 0 when no sessions
exceed the budget (or when no quarantine directory exists) and exits 1 on a
malformed or overflowing
.IR SIZE .
The
.I keep\-latest:N
selector lists sessions newest-first and purges every session after the first
.I N
(a decimal integer, N >= 1 (use all to purge everything)), exiting 0 when at most
.I N
sessions exist (or when no quarantine directory exists) and exiting 1 on a
malformed count.
Irreversible; the confirmation prompt names every target it would destroy, and is
skipped only when
.B \-\-force
is given. An explicit
.B \-\-dry\-run
prints selected session names and sizes under a \(lqWould purge\(rq heading,
including notes for held sessions skipped by bulk selectors, and exits 0 without
confirmation, deletion, a lock, or log/report writes, even with inherited
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_FORCE .
Held sessions still count toward size/count limits; a held named or latest
session is refused as in a real purge. A later
.BR \-\-clean ,
.BR \-\-force ,
or
.B \-\-interactive
cancels the preview, so \-\-dry\-run \-\-force purges.
JSON is not supported, including for previews. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
or
.BR \-\-doctor .
.TP
.B \-\-hold\-session \fISESSION\fR
Protect a quarantine session
.RI ( latest
or a timestamp, validated like
.BR \-\-restore )
from deletion by writing a private
.B .hold
marker into it, under the same lock as other mutating runs. The session's
modification time is left unchanged, so its retention age is not reset.
Bulk purge selectors
.RI ( all ", " older\-than:DAYS ", " max\-size:SIZE ", " keep\-latest:N )
and
.B \-\-trash\-keep\-days
retention skip a held session with a
.B Skipping held session
note, even with
.BR \-\-force ;
a held session still counts toward the
.I max\-size
budget and the
.I keep\-latest
count. A purge of a held
.I latest
or named session exits 1 instead of picking another session.
Any entry named
.B .hold
counts as a hold. A complete
.B \-\-restore
removes the marker with the session; a partial restore keeps both.
Holding a held session is a no-op. Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
.BR \-\-doctor ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
.BR \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-fail\-fast ,
.BR \-\-show\-matches ,
.BR \-\-exit\-code ,
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.BR \-\-clean ,
.BR \-\-interactive ,
or
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.B \-\-release\-session \fISESSION\fR
Remove the hold marker from a quarantine session
.RI ( latest
or a timestamp), so normal purge selectors and retention apply again based on
the session's original modification time. Releasing an unheld session is a no-op.
Cannot be combined with
.BR \-\-hold\-session ,
.BR \-\-restore ,
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-check\-config ,
.BR \-\-report ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-init\-config ,
.BR \-\-doctor ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-target ,
.BR \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ,
.BR \-\-min\-free\-path ,
.BR \-\-min\-age ,
.BR \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
.BR \-\-notify ,
.BR \-\-fail\-fast ,
.BR \-\-show\-matches ,
.BR \-\-exit\-code ,
.BR \-\-dry\-run ,
.BR \-\-clean ,
.BR \-\-interactive ,
or
.BR \-\-json .
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the tool version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display the usage message and exit.
.PP
Value-taking options
.RB ( \-c / \-\-config ,
.BR \-r / \-\-risk ,
.BR \-e / \-\-exclude ,
.BR \-\-restore ", " \-\-hold\-session ", " \-\-release\-session ,
.BR \-\-only ", " \-\-only\-exact ,
.BR \-\-only\-target ", " \-\-exclude\-target ", " \-\-only\-path ,
.BR \-\-exclude\-path ", " \-\-lock\-wait ", " \-\-min\-reclaim ,
.BR \-\-check\-health ,
.BR \-\-min\-free ", " \-\-min\-free\-path ", " \-\-min\-age ", " \-\-action\-timeout ,
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days ,
and
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions )
refuse a following long option
.RB ( \-\- ...)
or
.B \-d
as their argument and exit 1 with
.B "Error: \fI<flag>\fB requires a value, got option '\fI<token>\fB'."
before any analysis or deletion, so a missing value never turns a dry-run flag
into a pattern. Other dash-leading values, such as
.B \-x*
or a target NAME of
.BR \-n ,
are still accepted.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_CONFIG
When set to a non-empty path, uses that file as the configuration
(same as
.BR \-\-config ).
An explicit
.B \-\-config
still overrides. When unset, the XDG / system / checkout search order is unchanged.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_LOG
If set to a path under
.BR $HOME ,
enables the operation log without
.BR \-\-log .
The path accepts the same
.BR $HOME ", " ~ ", " $USER ,
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME ", " $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
and
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
placeholders as configuration targets. A path outside
.BR $HOME
is refused and logging is disabled.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_QUIET
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-quiet
(suppress per-target status lines). No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_DEBUG
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-debug
(show per-target FOUND, CLEAN and PROTECTED lines). No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_NOTIFY
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-notify
(send a desktop notification summarizing the cleanup result; no-op on dry-run). Non-cleanup modes ignore it. No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_FAIL_FAST
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-fail\-fast
(stop after the first failed cleanup target and skip inline retention).
Also accepts TRUE and YES, like LINUX_CLEANER_NOTIFY. Non-cleanup modes ignore it.
No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_JSON
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-json .
Valid with dry-run,
.BR \-\-list\-targets ,
.BR \-\-list\-sessions ,
or
.BR \-\-report ;
No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_ONLY_TARGET
When set to a non-empty pattern (or comma-separated list; empty entries are rejected), enables
.BR \-\-only\-target
(substring match on config target
.BR NAME ).
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_EXCLUDE_TARGET
When set to a non-empty pattern (or comma-separated list; empty entries are rejected), enables
.BR \-\-exclude\-target
(substring match on config target
.BR NAME ).
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_ONLY_PATH
When set to a non-empty existing absolute literal path without line feeds, enables
.BR \-\-only\-path
for cleanup or target inventory. Unset or empty means no scope. No placeholder
expansion is performed. CLI overrides even malformed inherited values. Excludes
action:* rows and cannot combine with session/config/report modes or global
retention (including
.BR LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS ).
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_EXCLUDE_PATH
When set to a non-empty existing absolute literal path without line feeds, enables
.BR \-\-exclude\-path
for cleanup or target inventory (skipping targets whose resolved physical or
expanded literal path equals, lies below, or contains the path). Unset or empty
means no exclusion. CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_LOCK_WAIT
Literal decimal 0..2147483647 seconds for
.BR \-\-lock\-wait .
Unset/empty means zero; leading zeros are normalized. Last CLI overrides even
malformed inherited input. Valid settings do not lock nonlocking modes.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_RECLAIM
When set to a non-empty size threshold (such as
.B 500M
or
.BR 2G ),
enables
.BR \-\-min\-reclaim .
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_FREE
When set to a non-empty size threshold (such as
.BR 10G ),
enables
.BR \-\-min\-free
(measured on the selected filesystem, default:
.BR HOME ).
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_FREE_PATH
When set to a non-empty absolute existing path, selects its filesystem for
.BR \-\-min\-free .
Requires a CLI or environment min-free threshold. Unset or empty uses HOME.
Invalid paths exit 1; failed probes warn and cleanup proceeds.
CLI
.B \-\-min\-free\-path
overrides this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_MIN_AGE
When set to a non-empty non-negative integer day count, enables
.BR \-\-min\-age .
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_ACTION_TIMEOUT
When set to a non-empty non-negative integer second count, enables
.BR \-\-action\-timeout .
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH_KEEP_DAYS
When set to a non-empty non-negative integer day count, enables
.BR \-\-trash\-keep\-days .
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_RISK
When set to
.BR safe ", " medium ", or " danger ,
sets the default
.BR \-\-risk
ceiling. CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-trash
(quarantine mode). No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_EXCLUDE
When set to a non-empty comma-separated glob list, enables
.BR \-\-exclude .
CLI flags override this variable.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_FORCE
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-force
(non-interactive clean). No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_YES_DANGER
When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
enables
.BR \-\-yes\-danger
(bypass danger confirmation). No CLI flag disables it; set it to 0 or unset it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_METRICS_FILE
When set to a literal absolute file path without line feeds, every non-dry-run
cleanup atomically writes a Prometheus
.B node_exporter
textfile there after saving
.I last\-report.txt
with
.B # HELP
and
.B # TYPE
lines for
.BR linux_cleaner_last_run_timestamp_seconds ,
.B linux_cleaner_last_run_success
(0 when any operation failed or the run was interrupted),
.BR linux_cleaner_targets{result="cleaned|skipped|failed"} ,
and
.BR linux_cleaner_reclaimed_bytes{kind="quarantined|permanent"} .
Environment-only (no CLI flag). A relative path, line feed, symlinked target or
parent directory, or write error prints a warning on stderr without failing the
run. Dry-run and read-only modes never write the file.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_PREVIEW_METRICS_FILE
Environment-only (no CLI flag). When set, a cleanup dry run (text or JSON)
atomically writes a Prometheus textfile with HELP and TYPE lines for three gauges:
.BR linux_cleaner_preview_timestamp_seconds
(Unix timestamp of the preview),
.BR linux_cleaner_preview_reclaimable_bytes
(the planned reclaimable total), and
.BR linux_cleaner_preview_would_skip
(1 for a minimum-reclaim/free skip or nothing to clean, otherwise 0).
Requires a literal absolute file path without line feeds and an existing writable
directory. Symlinked targets or parent directories are refused. Invalid paths,
write errors, or a path equal to
.B LINUX_CLEANER_METRICS_FILE
warn on stderr without failing the run. Unset or empty writes nothing.
Mutating cleanup, restore, purge, list, report, check-config, init-config, and
doctor modes ignore it.
.TP
.B LINUX_CLEANER_ALLOW_OUTSIDE_HOME
Deprecated no-op. When set to
.BR 1 ", " true ", or " yes ,
prints one warning. Targets outside
.BR $HOME
are always allowed.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to any non-empty value, disables ANSI color codes in output (see
.UR https://no-color.org
.UE ).
.SH CONFIGURATION
Targets are pipe-delimited lines:
.PP
.nf
RISK_LEVEL|NAME|PATH_EXPRESSION|DESCRIPTION|MIN_AGE_DAYS|CHECK_PROCESSES|CUSTOM_ACTION
.fi
.PP
The first five fields are required; the last two are optional. A malformed line is
rejected with a line-numbered error.
.PP
.I PATH_EXPRESSION
resolves the
.BR $HOME ,
.BR ~ ,
.BR $USER ,
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
and
.B $XDG_STATE_HOME
placeholders (when an XDG variable is unset or set to a relative path, it falls
back to its XDG Base Directory specification default:
.IR $HOME/.cache ,
.IR $HOME/.config ,
.IR $HOME/.local/share ,
or
.IR $HOME/.local/state ),
and may also contain
.B *
or
.B ?
wildcards. Paths containing either wildcard cannot contain
.B [
or
.BR ] ;
this also applies after placeholder expansion, when such a row is skipped with a
warning. Brackets remain literal in paths without wildcards.
A wildcard line expands to one independent target per matching
directory, each passing the same safety checks as a literal path. Matching is
performed by
.BR find (1)
below the literal part of the pattern and at exactly the depth the pattern
describes, never by shell globbing and never through
.BR eval .
A
.B CHECK_PROCESSES
token written as
.BI cmdline: pattern
matches the full command line rather than the process name, which is required for a
tool launched through a shared runtime (Electron or node), where the process does not
run under its own name. Prefer an installation path over a bare word. The cleaner's own
process ancestry, its descendants, and already-exited PIDs are excluded, so a pattern
appearing in its own invocation never blocks a target.
Targets with CHECK_PROCESSES require
.BR pgrep (1)
(usually from procps or procps-ng). A missing or failed query, including an invalid
regular expression or partial results with an error, skips that target before sizing,
sudo, actions or deletion, with a process-check failure reason. Other targets continue
under the normal skip and exit policy; targets without process checks need no pgrep.
.PP
The
.B CUSTOM_ACTION
field also accepts
.BR filter:cxx_build ,
which keeps an expanded wildcard match only when the directory holds a build-system
marker
.RB ( CMakeCache.txt ", " CMakeFiles ", " build.ninja ", " meson-info ,
.BR compile_commands.json ", or " .qmake.stash )
directly or one level below, so a source directory whose name merely begins with
"build" is excluded.
The same filter applies to literal paths without wildcards; surrounding whitespace
in the field is ignored and never disables the filter.
When a
.B filter:cxx_build
target has a positive
.BR MIN_AGE_DAYS ,
that age is an idle threshold for the directory as a whole: if anything under the
target was modified within
.B MIN_AGE_DAYS
days, the target is treated as clean
.RB ( "active within " \fIN\fR " days" )
and left untouched; otherwise the whole directory is sized and removed at once
.RB ( "(idle > " \fIN\fR " days)"
in the plan report).
.PP
The field also accepts
.BR exclude:NAME[,NAME...] .
These comma-separated patterns add to the global
.BR \-\-exclude " / " LINUX_CLEANER_EXCLUDE
patterns for this target only, using the same find glob/path semantics.
For example,
.B exclude:notes
preserves matching notes directories and all their contents while old session
logs can be cleaned. Dry-run sizes, deletion, quarantine, and empty-directory
cleanup honor the exclusions. With MIN_AGE_DAYS 0, files are processed individually
and the target directory remains. Wildcard rows retain normal ancestor pruning.
Empty or blank patterns and patterns containing pipes or newlines are invalid.
Field 7 holds one token: exclusions, filters, and actions cannot be combined.
.PP
Wildcard matches containing newlines, a pipe character, leading/trailing whitespace,
or literal placeholders that would expand again are skipped with a warning. These
names cannot be represented unchanged in the config record format; ordinary spaces
inside directory names remain supported.
.PP
A path component written as
.B **
matches one or more intervening directories, so one line covers a directory
class that occurs at several depths; that recursion is bounded at 10 levels
below the literal prefix. A pattern matching nothing contributes no target and
is not an error; a wildcard in the first path component is left unexpanded with
a warning rather than scanning the whole filesystem. Use
.B \-\-dry\-run
or
.B \-\-list\-targets
to enumerate every expanded match before cleaning.
.PP
A two-field directive of the form
.B protect|\fIPATH\fR
(first field literally
.BR protect ,
case-sensitive) protects a directory tree from cleanup.
.I PATH
expands
.BR $HOME ,
.BR ~ ,
.BR $USER ,
.BR $XDG_CACHE_HOME ,
.BR $XDG_CONFIG_HOME ,
.BR $XDG_DATA_HOME ,
and
.BR $XDG_STATE_HOME
(relative XDG values fall back to the spec default),
is physically canonicalized, and need not exist; an empty path, a relative path
after expansion, a path containing glob characters
.RB ( * ", " ? ", " [ ),
or extra fields is rejected with a
.IR config : line :
error. Protected lines are not listed by
.B \-\-list\-targets
or counted as targets by
.BR \-\-check\-config .
Any target whose canonical path equals a protected path, lies under one, or contains
one (whole path components only) is skipped before sizing with status
.BR protected ,
shown under
.B \-\-debug
as
.B [PROTECTED]
and grouped in the plan report under
.BR "Protected (by config):" .
.PP
The default config is resolved from an explicit
.BR \-c ,
then the XDG user config, then
.IR /etc/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf ,
then a
.I cleaner.conf
beside the script.
.PP
Predefined
.B CUSTOM_ACTION
values (enumerated whitelist only):
.RS
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:paccache_keep1 ", " action:paccache_keep2 ", " action:paccache_keep3
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:pacman_clean
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:apt_clean
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:dnf_clean
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:zypper_clean
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:apk_clean
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:uv_cache_prune
runs \fBuv cache prune \-\-cache\-dir\fR against the target's configured path
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:pnpm_store_prune
runs \fBpnpm store prune \-\-store\-dir\fR against the target's configured path
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:hf_cache_prune
runs \fBhf cache prune \-\-yes \-\-cache\-dir\fR against the target's configured path
.IP \(bu 3
.BR action:nix_store_gc
runs \fBnix\-store \-\-gc\fR as the invoking user
.RE
.SH SAFETY
Deletions are blocked for the filesystem root, system directories
.RI ( /usr ", " /etc ", " /var ", " /lib ", " /opt ", " /srv ", " /tmp ", " /run ", " /mnt ", " /media ", "
and similar), and for
.BR $HOME
itself or any target containing
.BR $HOME .
Paths outside
.BR $HOME
are allowed. Symlinked targets are refused. Paths are physically canonicalized to
identify their actual destinations before safety checks; a \fB..\fR component or
symlinked parent may resolve to an eligible destination outside \fB$HOME\fR. A target
that needs root is skipped, with a
warning, when those privileges are not available; a run with no terminal on stdin
never prompts for a password. User-defined
.B protect|\fIPATH\fR
lines in the configuration skip any target that equals, lies under, or contains a
protected path.
The cleaner always protects
.BR $HOME/.cache/linux\-cleaner
by the same canonical-path checks, even before it exists, without requiring a
.B protect
line. Targets equal to, under, or containing it (including
.B $HOME/.cache
and wildcard matches) are skipped as
.B protected
in text and JSON previews and cleanup, even with
.BR \-\-force .
This preserves quarantine sessions, operations.log, and the live cleaner.lock.
With Git available, targets containing git-tracked files are skipped even with \-\-force, unless the cleaner runs as root or the target is unreadable without sudo.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf
XDG user configuration.
.TP
.I /etc/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.conf
System configuration.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.lock
Exclusive (non-blocking by default, or bounded by
.BR \-\-lock\-wait )
.BR flock (1)
lock held by mutating runs
.RB ( \-\-clean / \-\-force / \-\-interactive ,
.BR \-\-restore ,
and
.BR \-\-purge\-sessions ).
The directory is created mode 0700 and symlinks are refused.
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/quarantine/
Quarantine sessions for
.BR \-\-trash / \-\-restore / \-\-purge\-sessions .
.TP
.I ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/operations.log
Default operation log when
.B \-\-log
or
.B LINUX_CLEANER_LOG
is enabled and no explicit path is given.
.TP
.I ${XDG_STATE_HOME:\-$HOME/.local/state}/linux\-cleaner/last\-report.txt
Last cleanup report (plan and result, without color codes), rewritten by every
non-dry-run cleanup, including one with nothing to clean. The directory is created mode
0700. A symlink at the directory or the file is not followed. Printed by
.BR \-\-report .
.TP
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-clean.service
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-clean.timer
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-retention.service
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-retention.timer
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-monitor.service
.I /usr/lib/systemd/user/linux-cleaner-monitor.timer
Systemd user units installed by
.B make install
or
.B install.sh
with
.B PREFIX=/usr
(mode 0644). Install does not enable them. The clean unit runs
.B cleaner \-\-clean \-\-force
and deletes permanently unless
.B LINUX_CLEANER_TRASH=1
is set in
.IR ~/.config/linux-cleaner/env .
Before uninstalling or removing the package, each user who enabled the timers
should run
.B systemctl \-\-user disable \-\-now linux\-cleaner\-clean.timer linux\-cleaner\-retention.timer linux\-cleaner\-monitor.timer
and, after removal,
.BR "systemctl \-\-user daemon\-reload" .
.TP
.I /usr/share/bash\-completion/completions/cleaner
.I /usr/share/zsh/site\-functions/_cleaner
.I /usr/share/fish/vendor_completions.d/cleaner.fish
Shell completions for bash, zsh and fish, installed by both installers with
.B PREFIX=/usr
(mode 0644).
.SH JOURNAL ERROR PRIORITY
Failed cleanups emit one
.B <3>linux\-cleaner:
summary on stderr with the failed target count and names. Interrupted cleanups
name the signal; early non-zero cleanup exits name the exit status.
A failed
.B \-\-purge\-sessions
run (the retention unit), including a lock timeout or an invalid selector,
names its selector and exit status. A failed dry run (the monitor unit) says the run stopped
before cleaning. A failed
.B \-\-trash\-keep\-days
purge after a skipped cleanup says so, and after a finished cleanup the summary
ends with
.BR retention_failed=true .
This is independent of
.BR \-\-notify
and leaves exit statuses unchanged. The line is emitted only when
.B JOURNAL_STREAM
exactly matches stderr's device and inode. An unset or mismatched value, a failed
or unavailable
.BR stat (1),
or a successful run (including intentional status 2 from
.BR \-\-exit\-code )
emits no priority line. Terminal and redirected output
normally receive no such line. With systemd's default
.BR SyslogLevelPrefix=yes ,
the prefix gives the line error priority, visible with
.BR "journalctl \-\-user \-t linux\-cleaner \-p err" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
With
.BR \-\-report\ \-\-check\-health\ SECONDS ,
returns 0 only for a recent successful saved report (including skips), otherwise
1 with a one-line reason on stderr. Normal text or JSON output is unchanged.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-exit\-code ,
a cleanup dry run returns 2 when cleanup would run, or 0 when a minimum-reclaim
or minimum-free gate would skip it or there is nothing to clean. Errors still
return 1. Without this modifier, successful previews return 0.
.PP
Returns 0 on success, 130
.RB ( SIGINT )
or 143
.RB ( SIGTERM )
when a cleanup is interrupted by a signal (after printing and saving the partial
result report with an
.B "Interrupted: SIGINT|SIGTERM before all targets finished"
line and logging
.B "mode=run outcome=interrupted"
when logging is enabled), and non-zero (1) on a configuration/usage error, a concurrent
mutating run holding
.IR ~/.cache/linux\-cleaner/cleaner.lock ,
or a failed operation.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Preview what would be cleaned:
.B cleaner
.TP
Clean safe targets, moving files to quarantine:
.B cleaner \-\-trash \-\-clean
.TP
List and restore quarantined files:
.B cleaner \-\-list\-sessions
.br
.B cleaner \-\-restore latest
.TP
Reclaim space by purging all quarantine sessions:
.B cleaner \-\-purge\-sessions all \-\-force
.TP
Cron-friendly dry-run preview (2 when cleanup would run, 0 when it would skip):
.B cleaner \-\-quiet \-\-dry\-run \-\-exit\-code
.TP
Machine-readable dry-run summary for wrapper scripts:
.B cleaner \-\-json \-\-dry\-run
.TP
Preview cleanup for a single config target by name:
.B cleaner \-\-only\-target "NPM" \-\-dry\-run
.TP
Enable quiet mode via environment (same as
.BR \-\-quiet ):
.B LINUX_CLEANER_QUIET=1 cleaner \-\-dry\-run
.TP
Show per-target FOUND, CLEAN and PROTECTED lines via environment (same as
.BR \-\-debug ):
.B LINUX_CLEANER_DEBUG=1 cleaner \-\-dry\-run
.TP
Daily cleanup (permanent deletion of safe targets):
.B cleaner \-\-clean \-\-force
.TP
Opt-in quarantine, paired with weekly retention:
.B cleaner \-\-clean \-\-trash \-\-force
.br
.B cleaner \-\-purge\-sessions older\-than:30 \-\-force
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR paccache (8),
.BR find (1)
